  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable this afternoon into tonight with the \npotential for isolated to widely scattered showers or storms. \n\nThis afternoon, high pressure located over the eastern Great Lakes \ncontinues to ridge southward, holding a weak frontal boundary south \nof the VA/NC border. Isolated to widely scattered showers or storms \nremain possible throughout this afternoon into this evening. The \nbest thunderstorm potential is general along and south of the US-460 \ncorridor. Instability is much less impressive compared to yesterday, \nwith only ~1000-1500 J/kg of MLCAPE across southern portions of the \nforecast area. In addition, an onshore, NE wind (combined with cloud \ncover) has kept temperatures cooler today with readings in the mid \nto upper 80s. \n\nAny convection tapers off after sunset. Overnight, remnant decaying \nshowers from the Ohio Valley may try to drift towards far \nnorthern/western areas, but any measurable precipitation likely \nholds off until the daylight hours of Sunday. Otherwise, lows will \nrange from the upper 60s to low 70s under a partly cloudy sky.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Severe storms are possible on Sunday with SPC \nhighlighting much of the area in a Slight (2 out of 5) risk. \nDamaging wind gusts are the main threat, but an isolated tornado \nalso cannot be ruled out. In addition, there is a heavy rain and \nflooding threat with WPC highlighting much of the area in a Slight \nERO.\n\nAn unsettled to potentially active day is expected for Sunday. The \nexpansive upper ridge over the southern tier of the country begins \nto breakdown as another shortwave trough digs across the Ohio \nValley.  The surface boundary pushes back north as a warm front, \nopening the door for deep tropical moisture transport (PWATs \nclimbing to 2.3\"+). Recent 12z high-res guidance (specifically the \n3km NAM and RRFS) have hinted at a slower northward progression of \nthe warm front, potentially keeping northeastern portions of the \narea in a bit of a wedge. In addition, morning showers and clouds \nmay limit instability, at least initially. Still, SW of the front a \nvery unstable airmass is expected with MLCAPE values climbing to \npotentially 2000+ J/kg. \n\nWidespread shower and thunderstorm development is expected later \nSunday afternoon through Sunday evening. Deep layer shear of \n~25 to 30 knots will support multi-cells and bowing segments \ncapable of producing damaging wind gusts of 50 to 60+ mph and \neven some isolated hail (despite poor mid-level lapse rates). In\naddition, E- SE winds along the warm front and 0-1 km SRH \nvalues of 150 to 200 will allow for potential rotation and an \nisolated tornado threat. SPC has most of our Virginia counties \nhighlighted in a Slight Risk for severe storms, with a Marginal \nRisk for our Maryland and North Carolina counties. \n\nHeavy rainfall is also expected to be a threat on Sunday. Slow \nmoving or back building convection will produce torrential rainfall \nrates. WPC has expanded the Slight Risk ERO to cover a majority of \nthe area. Scattered instances of flash flooding will be possible, \nespecially across urban and poor drainage areas. Convection \ngradually weakens Sunday night.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Hot and humid with afternoon storms Monday ahead of \nthe next cold front.\n\nThe hot, humid conditions look to peak on Monday ahead of an \napproaching cold front. Highs will reach the low to mid 90s. The hot \ntemperatures combined with dewpoints in the mid to upper 70s will \nallow for heat indices of 105+ across southern/southeastern \nportions of the area and 100-105 elsewhere. Heat Advisories will\nlikely be needed for at least our North Carolina and southeast \nVirginia counties. Scattered showers and storms are also \nexpected Monday afternoon/evening along the frontal boundary. \nDrier and slightly cooler conditions arrive Tuesday into \nmidweek.",
